answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274012} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbee3054-bcc6-57fb-b4fc-f2616c6465d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274963}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e0baa0c-c163-5abd-bc09-5322350948ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-158052" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=-1,$ then $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+24\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 12) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $cos⁡(-x+24\\pi )=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275f4184-6614-5fb5-b945-c0a925615360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272735} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7df9b3-60b6-5ef6-8973-d6465d2aa3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273582} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1e5805-1c6c-5d57-b929-84303eb5f147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273626} {:answer/id #uuid "ae068c2c-1302-562e-8e3f-5924d2a8dd0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273888} {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea032dc8-afc8-5574-9882-b42bbb63200d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275047}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a595d8eb-425e-5670-9569-3c6cc685c869" :question/math-academy-id "q-158059" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=0,$ then $\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric over the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+18\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pi \\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}]}
